rows=[(689, 'a-pause-with-the-bicycle', 'A pause before the sky', 'The poem calls hunger an ordinary familiar person arriving by bicycle and looking at the sky, before widening hunger into war, love and poetry. Choose that humane everyday pause, without implying a particular real person, deprivation or documentary evidence.', 'An original horizontal literary watercolor with loose graphite. A modest Indian verandah viewed from just outside at a quiet oblique angle. One anonymous adult in a plain loose shirt and trousers has stopped beside an ordinary roadster bicycle leaned securely against a low wall. The small figure occupies the far-left third, viewed mostly from behind in a relaxed three-quarter back view, head slightly raised towards a broad ordinary cloudy sky beyond the verandah. No face detail, likeness or named identity. Natural adult proportions, feet planted, hands loosely resting at the low wall rather than on complicated bicycle parts. Bicycle partly cropped left, only necessary simple wheel and frame geometry visible. The right half opens into pale rain-cleared daylight. Worn-by-use but cared-for surfaces, muted warm walls and cool dilute blue-grey sky. A pause belonging to everyday life, not poverty theatre, heroic yearning or a staged cinematic scene. Human-made uneven watercolor edges and a few economical pencil marks, no all-over sharpness, decorative vegetation, lettering or symbolic collage.'), (683, 'water-finds-a-way', 'Water finding its way', 'The Hindi poem asks how the life-current sustains courage and nourishes others wherever it travels. Show one modest act of water reaching living ground, without a desert-mountain-season montage.', 'Original horizontal watercolor and restrained colored-pencil study, seen almost from above: a shallow irregular irrigation runnel in firm brown garden soil, entering at upper left, bending naturally through the middle and dividing into two narrower channels near the right edge. Only a few small young green shoots grow along damp edges, with differing natural spacing. The water is shallow and slightly clouded, with subtle reflected pale daylight and darker wet earth directly touching it; flow follows the lower channel bed. Large unpainted ivory-paper gaps around loosely described soil. Matter-of-fact intimate scale, optimistic without glowing magical seedlings or a lush idyllic landscape. Sparse warm earth washes with tiny clear green accents and broken pencil edges. No hands, buckets, people, river vista, mountains, sunbeams, text or logos. Avoid uniform seedling ranks, symmetrical branching, jewel-like droplets and decorative distress.'), (687, 'within-the-dark-wash', 'Within the dark wash', 'Dark Matters After All finds rest and creative possibility in darkness. Use an original nonfigurative ink-wash field as an emotional accompaniment; do not copy the named painters, their works, signature circles or drip technique, and do not illustrate a scientific origin claim.', 'An original horizontal nonfigurative ink-and-watercolor study on opaque warm ivory paper. A broad irregular midnight indigo wash drifts from the cropped left edge into muted violet-grey, with a narrow unpainted pale opening that is slightly offset and angled near the right third. The painted mass is soft and quiet, uneven in density where water naturally gathers, with two or three restrained overlapping brush boundaries and extensive resting space at the lower-right. The pale opening is simply reserved paper, not a glowing beam or galaxy. No perfect circle, central dot, concentric halo, cosmic stars, splatter, drip web, faces, words or identifiable scene. This is a new subdued study of dark and breathing space, not a reproduction or pastiche of any named painting. Preserve absorption and selective pigment variation without mechanical noise, fake paper grain, theatrical contrast or busy decoration.')]
items=[]
